/* KCNA content - Cloud Native Application Delivery (16% of exam). Auto-generated. */
KCNA.register({"id": "delivery", "name": "Cloud Native Application Delivery", "weight": 16, "notes": [{"id": "delivery-note-1", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "title": "Application Delivery Fundamentals", "html": "<p>Application delivery is the end-to-end practice of getting code from a developer's machine into a running, observable workload in a cluster, repeatedly and safely.</p><ul><li><strong>Build</strong>: source code is compiled and packaged into an immutable <strong>container image</strong>, then pushed to a <strong>registry</strong> (e.g. Docker Hub, GHCR, Harbor).</li><li><strong>Release/Deploy</strong>: the image is referenced by a Kubernetes manifest (<code>Deployment</code>, <code>StatefulSet</code>) and applied to the cluster.</li><li><strong>Declarative model</strong>: you declare the <em>desired state</em> in YAML; controllers reconcile actual state toward it. This is the foundation of all cloud native delivery.</li><li><strong>Immutability</strong>: never patch a running container. Build a new image with a new tag and roll it out. Avoid the <code>latest</code> tag in production (non-deterministic).</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Exam tip — deployment strategies</strong>: <strong>Rolling update</strong> (default for <code>Deployment</code>, gradual replacement, no downtime); <strong>Recreate</strong> (kill all, then start new, causes downtime); <strong>Blue-Green</strong> (two full environments, switch traffic); <strong>Canary</strong> (route a small % of traffic to the new version first).</li><li><strong>Rollbacks</strong>: <code>Deployment</code> keeps revision history; <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> reverts.</li><li><strong>12-Factor App</strong> principles (config in env, stateless processes, disposability) underpin cloud native delivery.</li><li>Goal of good delivery: <strong>repeatability, reliability, speed, and safe rollback</strong>.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "delivery-note-2", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD concepts and pipelines", "title": "CI/CD Concepts and Pipelines", "html": "<p>CI/CD automates the path from a code commit to a deployed workload, replacing manual, error-prone steps with a repeatable pipeline.</p><ul><li><strong>CI (Continuous Integration)</strong>: every commit is automatically built and tested. Catches integration bugs early, keeps <code>main</code> always releasable.</li><li><strong>CD</strong> has two meanings: <strong>Continuous Delivery</strong> (every change is automatically prepared/staged but a human approves the production release) vs <strong>Continuous Deployment</strong> (every passing change goes to production with no manual gate).</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Typical pipeline stages</strong>: source/commit &rarr; build image &rarr; test (unit/integration) &rarr; scan (vulnerabilities, lint) &rarr; push to registry &rarr; deploy.</li><li><strong>Pipeline as code</strong>: the pipeline definition lives in the repo (e.g. <code>.gitlab-ci.yml</code>, GitHub Actions workflow), versioned alongside the app.</li><li><strong>CNCF tools</strong>: <strong>Tekton</strong> (Kubernetes-native CI/CD using <code>Task</code> and <code>Pipeline</code> CRDs, runs each step as a pod), <strong>Argo Workflows</strong>, plus ecosystem tools like Jenkins, GitLab CI, GitHub Actions.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Exam tip</strong>: CI/CD is <strong>push-based</strong> — the pipeline has credentials and pushes changes <em>into</em> the cluster (<code>kubectl apply</code>). Contrast with GitOps, which is <strong>pull-based</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tekton vs Argo Workflows</strong>: both run pipelines as pods in-cluster; Tekton focuses on CI/CD building blocks, Argo Workflows is a general DAG/workflow engine.</li><li>Benefits: faster feedback, fewer manual errors, consistent and auditable releases.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "delivery-note-3", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "title": "GitOps: Argo CD & Flux", "html": "<p><strong>GitOps</strong> uses a Git repository as the single source of truth for declarative infrastructure and apps; an in-cluster agent continuously reconciles the cluster to match Git.</p><ul><li><strong>Four GitOps principles</strong>: (1) <strong>Declarative</strong> — desired state expressed as data; (2) <strong>Versioned & immutable</strong> — stored in Git; (3) <strong>Pulled automatically</strong> — agents pull approved state; (4) <strong>Continuously reconciled</strong> — drift is detected and corrected.</li><li><strong>Pull-based</strong>: the agent runs <em>inside</em> the cluster and pulls from Git. The cluster's credentials never leave it — more secure than push-based CI/CD. <strong>Exam tip</strong>: GitOps = pull; classic CI/CD = push.</li><li><strong>Reconciliation loop</strong>: agent compares Git (desired) vs cluster (actual), reports <em>drift</em>, and applies changes. Reverting a bad change = a Git revert.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Argo CD</strong>: GitOps controller with a rich UI/CLI. Core CRD is <code>Application</code> (maps a Git path to a cluster/namespace). Shows sync status (Synced/OutOfSync) and health; supports manual or automated sync and self-heal.</li><li><strong>Flux</strong> (CNCF graduated): a set of controllers (GitOps Toolkit) — <code>GitRepository</code>, <code>Kustomization</code>, <code>HelmRelease</code>. CLI-driven, no built-in UI; composable and lightweight.</li><li>Both are <strong>CNCF graduated</strong> and natively support <strong>Helm</strong> and <strong>Kustomize</strong> sources.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Benefits</strong>: auditable Git history, easy rollback, drift correction, separation of CI (build/push image) from CD (GitOps deploys).</li></ul>"}, {"id": "delivery-note-4", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging and templating with Helm and Kustomize", "title": "Helm & Kustomize Packaging", "html": "<p>Helm and Kustomize both produce Kubernetes manifests but take opposite approaches: Helm <strong>templates</strong> and packages, Kustomize <strong>overlays</strong> plain YAML.</p><ul><li><strong>Helm</strong> is the <em>package manager</em> for Kubernetes (CNCF graduated). A package is a <strong>chart</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chart structure</strong>: <code>Chart.yaml</code> (metadata), <code>values.yaml</code> (default config), and <code>templates/</code> (Go-templated manifests using <code>{{ .Values.x }}</code>).</li><li><strong>Release</strong>: an installed instance of a chart; Helm v3 tracks release state as Secrets in-cluster (no Tiller — removed in v3).</li><li>Key commands: <code>helm install</code>, <code>helm upgrade</code>, <code>helm rollback</code>, <code>helm template</code> (render only). Charts are shared via chart <strong>repositories</strong> or OCI registries.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Kustomize</strong> is <strong>template-free</strong>; built into <code>kubectl</code> (<code>kubectl apply -k</code>).</li><li>Uses a <strong>base + overlays</strong> model driven by <code>kustomization.yaml</code>: a shared base is customized per environment (dev/staging/prod) via patches, <code>namePrefix</code>, <code>commonLabels</code>, and image overrides — without copying YAML.</li></ul><ul><li><strong>Exam tip</strong>: Helm = templating + packaging + versioned releases + lifecycle management; Kustomize = patch/overlay plain YAML, no templating language, native to kubectl.</li><li>They can be combined; both Argo CD and Flux natively support each.</li></ul>"}], "flashcards": [{"id": "delivery-fc-1", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "front": "In cloud native delivery, what is the difference between imperative and declarative configuration?", "back": "Imperative tells the system the exact commands/steps to run to reach a state (e.g. 'kubectl run'). Declarative describes the desired end state in manifests and lets Kubernetes controllers reconcile actual state toward it. Kubernetes is fundamentally declarative."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-2", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "front": "What is a Kubernetes controller's reconciliation loop?", "back": "A control loop that continuously observes the cluster's actual state, compares it to the desired state stored in etcd, and takes action to converge actual toward desired. This is the core mechanism behind self-healing and declarative delivery."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-3", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "front": "What does 'desired state' mean and where is it stored in Kubernetes?", "back": "Desired state is the intended configuration of objects (replicas, image, config) declared by the user. The API server persists it in etcd, the cluster's key-value datastore, which acts as the single source of truth."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-4", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "front": "What is the role of a container registry in application delivery?", "back": "A container registry stores and distributes versioned container images (e.g. Docker Hub, GHCR, Harbor). CI builds and pushes images there; the kubelet pulls them at deploy time based on the image reference in the pod spec."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-5", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Application delivery fundamentals", "front": "What is the difference between rolling update and recreate deployment strategies in Kubernetes?", "back": "RollingUpdate (the default for Deployments) incrementally replaces old pods with new ones to maintain availability. Recreate terminates all old pods first, then creates new ones, causing downtime but avoiding two versions running simultaneously."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-6", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD concepts and pipelines", "front": "What do CI and CD stand for, and how do they differ?", "back": "CI is Continuous Integration: frequently merging code and automatically building/testing it. CD is Continuous Delivery (changes are automatically prepared/validated for release, with a manual gate to production) or Continuous Deployment (every passing change is automatically released to production with no manual gate)."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-7", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD concepts and pipelines", "front": "What is a CI/CD pipeline?", "back": "An automated sequence of stages (build, test, scan, package, deploy) that code passes through from commit to production. Each stage gates progression, providing fast feedback and consistent, repeatable releases."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-8", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD concepts and pipelines", "front": "What is Tekton in the CNCF CI/CD ecosystem?", "back": "Tekton is a CNCF Kubernetes-native CI/CD framework that defines pipelines as Kubernetes Custom Resources (Tasks, Pipelines, PipelineRuns). Each step runs in its own container/pod, so pipelines execute directly on the cluster."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-9", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD concepts and pipelines", "front": "Why are container images typically tagged immutably (e.g. by Git SHA or version) in CI/CD?", "back": "Immutable, unique tags ensure each build is uniquely identifiable and reproducible, so deployments are traceable and rollbacks are reliable. Mutable tags like 'latest' are discouraged because they can silently change what gets deployed."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-10", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "front": "What is GitOps?", "back": "An operating model where a Git repository is the single source of truth for declarative infrastructure and application config. An in-cluster agent continuously reconciles the live cluster state to match Git, so changes are made via Git commits/PRs rather than direct kubectl commands."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-11", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "front": "What is the difference between push-based and pull-based delivery, and which does GitOps use?", "back": "Push-based: an external CI system pushes changes into the cluster (needs cluster credentials outside it). Pull-based: an agent inside the cluster pulls desired state from Git and applies it. GitOps favors the pull-based model for better security and drift correction."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-12", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "front": "What is Argo CD and what is its core custom resource?", "back": "Argo CD is a CNCF declarative GitOps continuous delivery tool for Kubernetes. Its central CR is the Application, which maps a Git source (repo/path/revision) to a destination cluster/namespace, and it shows sync status (Synced/OutOfSync) and health."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-13", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "front": "What is Flux (Flux CD)?", "back": "Flux is a CNCF GitOps toolkit for Kubernetes built from composable controllers (source-controller, kustomize-controller, helm-controller, notification-controller). It continuously pulls from Git/Helm/OCI sources and reconciles the cluster to the declared state."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-14", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps with Argo CD and Flux (declarative, pull-based reconciliation)", "front": "In GitOps, what is configuration drift and how is it handled?", "back": "Drift is when the live cluster state diverges from the desired state in Git (e.g. a manual kubectl edit). The GitOps agent detects this (marks it OutOfSync) and, if auto-sync/self-heal is enabled, reverts the cluster back to match Git."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-15", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging and templating with Helm and Kustomize", "front": "What is Helm and what is a Helm chart?", "back": "Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes. A chart is a versioned bundle of templated Kubernetes manifests plus metadata; installing a chart with a set of values creates a release. Charts enable reusable, parameterized, distributable application packaging."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-16", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging and templating with Helm and Kustomize", "front": "What is the role of values.yaml in a Helm chart?", "back": "values.yaml supplies the default configuration values that Helm injects into the chart's Go templates at render time. Users override these values (via --set or a custom values file) to customize a release without editing the templates themselves."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-17", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging and templating with Helm and Kustomize", "front": "What is Kustomize and how does it differ fundamentally from Helm?", "back": "Kustomize is a template-free tool (built into kubectl via -k) that customizes plain YAML using a base plus overlays that patch it per environment. Unlike Helm, it uses no templating language or variable substitution -- it layers declarative patches over existing manifests."}, {"id": "delivery-fc-18", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging and templating with Helm and Kustomize", "front": "In Kustomize, what is the relationship between a 'base' and an 'overlay'?", "back": "A base holds common, environment-agnostic manifests. An overlay references that base and applies patches (e.g. replica counts, image tags, namespaces) for a specific environment like dev or prod, promoting reuse without duplicating full YAML."}], "questions": [{"id": "delivery-q-1", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Declarative vs Imperative", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Kubernetes is fundamentally built around a declarative model for application delivery. What does this mean in practice?", "options": ["You declare the desired end state, and controllers work to make actual state match it", "You issue a precise ordered sequence of commands that the cluster executes exactly once", "You must manually restart Pods whenever their actual state drifts from what you intended", "The API server only accepts configuration changes during a scheduled maintenance window"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "In the declarative model you describe the desired state (e.g., in a manifest), and Kubernetes controllers continuously reconcile actual state toward it. Issuing an exact step-by-step sequence of commands is the imperative model, which is the opposite of how Kubernetes is designed to operate.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-kubernetes-objects/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-2", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Self-healing / Reconciliation", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "An application is defined with a Deployment requesting 3 replicas. A node fails and one of its Pods is lost. What does Kubernetes do automatically?", "options": ["It schedules a replacement Pod to restore the count back to 3 replicas", "It waits for an administrator to manually re-create the missing Pod", "It permanently scales the Deployment down to 2 replicas to match reality", "It deletes the Deployment because its desired state can no longer be met"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A Deployment manages a ReplicaSet whose controller continuously reconciles actual state to the desired replica count, so it creates a replacement Pod to restore the requested 3 replicas. This self-healing behavior is automatic and requires no manual intervention or permanent downscaling.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/controller/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-3", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Managing Workloads with kubectl", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Which kubectl command applies the configuration defined in a manifest file to a cluster, creating or updating resources to match it?", "options": ["kubectl apply -f app.yaml", "kubectl get -f app.yaml", "kubectl describe -f app.yaml", "kubectl logs -f app.yaml"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "kubectl apply -f applies a manifest declaratively, creating resources or reconciling existing ones toward the file's desired state. 'get' and 'describe' only read state, and 'logs' streams container logs (its -f flag means 'follow', not 'file').", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_apply/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-4", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Packaging with Helm", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A team wants to package a multi-resource application (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap) into a single versioned, templated, reusable unit that can be installed and upgraded with configurable values. Which CNCF tool is purpose-built for this?", "options": ["Helm, using a versioned chart", "containerd, using an image", "etcd, using a cluster snapshot", "kube-proxy, using an iptables rule"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Helm packages related Kubernetes manifests into a versioned, templated chart with configurable values, making install and upgrade repeatable. containerd is a container runtime, etcd is the cluster datastore, and kube-proxy handles Service networking; none of those package applications for delivery.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-5", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which statement best describes the core principle of GitOps for application delivery?", "options": ["A Git repository holds the desired state, and an agent reconciles the cluster to match it", "Developers SSH directly into cluster nodes to push application updates by hand", "Configuration lives only in the cluster's etcd and is never put under version control", "Container images are built inside the Kubernetes API server on every commit to main"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "GitOps uses a Git repo as the declarative source of truth, with an automated agent (e.g., Argo CD or Flux) that continuously reconciles the live cluster to the committed state. Manual SSH pushes and unversioned, etcd-only configuration are exactly the practices GitOps replaces.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-6", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Controllers and Operators", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "How does the Operator pattern extend Kubernetes' native application delivery model?", "options": ["It packages operational knowledge into a custom controller that reconciles Custom Resources", "It replaces the kube-scheduler so Pods are placed without considering resource requests", "It is a standalone GUI that submits manifests to the API server on a human's behalf", "It bypasses the Kubernetes API server and writes desired state directly into etcd storage"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "An Operator combines Custom Resource Definitions with a custom controller that encodes app-specific operational logic, extending the reconciliation model to automate complex Day-2 operations like backups, scaling, and upgrades. It does not replace the scheduler, act as a mere GUI, or bypass the API server to write to etcd directly.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-7", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD Fundamentals", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "In a CI/CD pipeline, what does the \"CI\" (Continuous Integration) stage primarily focus on?", "options": ["Frequently merging developers' changes into a shared repo, then building and testing them", "Automatically deploying every approved release to production servers without human review", "Continuously monitoring running production workloads for latency and performance regressions", "Provisioning the underlying cloud infrastructure that the application will eventually run on"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Continuous Integration is about merging code changes often into a shared mainline and automatically building and testing them to catch integration problems early. Automated deployment to production describes Continuous Deployment, and infrastructure provisioning and production monitoring are separate concerns.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-integration/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-8", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Continuous Delivery vs. Deployment", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What is the key distinction between Continuous Delivery and Continuous Deployment?", "options": ["Continuous Delivery keeps every change release-ready but gates production on a manual approval", "Continuous Delivery only builds artifacts, while Continuous Deployment only runs the test suite", "Continuous Delivery applies to stateless apps, while Continuous Deployment covers stateful ones", "Continuous Delivery runs in a CI server, while Continuous Deployment runs only inside Kubernetes"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "In Continuous Delivery, every change that passes automated checks is kept deployable but a human gives final approval before production; in Continuous Deployment, every change that passes automated tests is released to production with no manual gate. The other options misstate the difference.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-delivery/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-9", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "In the GitOps approach to continuous delivery for Kubernetes, what serves as the single source of truth for the desired state of the cluster?", "options": ["A Git repository of declarative configuration manifests", "The kubelet's local object cache on each worker node", "An imperative shell script run manually by an operator", "The container registry storing the application images"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "GitOps uses a Git repository of declarative manifests as the single source of truth; an agent (such as Argo CD or Flux) continuously reconciles the live cluster state to match Git. A container registry stores images, not the desired cluster state, and imperative scripts contradict the declarative GitOps model.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-10", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CNCF CD Tooling", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which pair of CNCF projects are commonly used to implement GitOps-based continuous delivery to Kubernetes?", "options": ["Argo CD and Flux", "Prometheus and Grafana", "Envoy and Linkerd", "etcd and CoreDNS"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Argo CD and Flux are CNCF continuous delivery tools that implement GitOps by reconciling cluster state with Git. Prometheus/Grafana are observability tools, Envoy/Linkerd handle networking and service mesh, and etcd/CoreDNS provide cluster data storage and DNS.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-11", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Pipeline Concepts", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "What is the main purpose of running an automated test suite as a stage within a CI/CD pipeline?", "options": ["To detect defects early and stop broken code from progressing toward production", "To increase the size of the final container image for redundancy across nodes", "To replace the need for a version control system in the delivery workflow", "To require manual approval of each commit before it can be merged to main"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Automated testing in a pipeline gives fast feedback so defects are caught early and faulty changes are stopped before they reach production. It does not replace version control, does not add image bloat, and automation is the opposite of manual per-commit approval.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-integration/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-12", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Reconciliation", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "A team uses a GitOps controller running inside the cluster. An operator manually edits a Deployment with kubectl, changing the replica count, but the value in the Git repository is unchanged. Assuming a typical pull-based GitOps setup with self-healing enabled, what is the expected behavior?", "options": ["The controller detects the drift and reconciles the Deployment back to the count in Git", "The manual change becomes the new desired state and is committed back to Git automatically", "The controller permanently disables itself because a manual change was detected in-cluster", "The cluster ignores Git from that point on and trusts only the manually applied value"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A pull-based GitOps controller with self-healing continuously reconciles live state to the desired state in Git, so out-of-band manual changes are treated as drift and reverted to match the repository. GitOps does not auto-commit live changes back to Git, nor does it abandon Git as the source of truth.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-13", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Principles", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "In a GitOps workflow, what serves as the single source of truth for the desired state of the cluster?", "options": ["A Git repository containing declarative configuration", "The live state stored in the cluster's etcd datastore", "A CI server's build artifacts and archived job logs", "An imperative shell script run by a cluster operator"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The defining GitOps principle is that a Git repository holds the declarative desired state and acts as the single source of truth. etcd reflects the live (actual) state that GitOps continuously reconciles toward the Git-declared desired state, so it is not the source of truth.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-14", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Reconciliation Model", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Tools like Argo CD and Flux are described as using a 'pull-based' deployment model. What does this mean?", "options": ["An in-cluster agent pulls desired state from Git and applies it", "A CI pipeline pushes manifests to the cluster using kubectl apply", "Developers manually pull images from a registry onto each node", "The cluster pulls container logs back to a central Git server"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "In the pull-based model, an agent running inside the cluster continuously pulls (fetches) the desired state from Git and reconciles the cluster to match it. This contrasts with push-based CI pipelines that use external credentials to push changes into the cluster.", "sourceUrl": "https://fluxcd.io/flux/concepts/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-15", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Drift Detection", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "An operator uses kubectl to manually scale a Deployment that is managed by Argo CD. What does Argo CD report about this application?", "options": ["The application is OutOfSync because live state differs from Git", "The application is automatically deleted to protect the cluster", "The change is committed back to Git to update the source of truth", "Nothing, because Argo CD only checks state at initial deployment"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Argo CD continuously compares live cluster state against the desired state in Git. A manual change creates drift, so the application is reported as OutOfSync. GitOps tools reconcile from Git to cluster; they do not write live changes back to Git.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/diffing/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-16", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Self-Healing", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "With automated sync and self-healing enabled, how does a GitOps controller respond when it detects that the live cluster state has drifted from the desired state in Git?", "options": ["It re-applies the manifests from Git to revert the cluster", "It updates the Git repository to match the live cluster state", "It pauses reconciliation until an operator manually approves", "It rolls back the Git history to the previous known commit"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Self-healing means the controller automatically re-applies the Git-declared desired state to correct drift, restoring the cluster to match Git. GitOps treats Git as authoritative, so it never overwrites Git with live cluster state.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-17", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Tooling", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Argo CD and Flux are both CNCF Graduated projects in which category of cloud native tooling?", "options": ["Continuous delivery / GitOps for Kubernetes", "Container runtimes (CRI implementations)", "Service mesh data plane proxies for pods", "Distributed tracing collection backends"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Argo CD and Flux are both CNCF Graduated projects focused on continuous delivery and GitOps for Kubernetes. They are not container runtimes, service meshes, or tracing systems.", "sourceUrl": "https://www.cncf.io/projects/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-18", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Security Model", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Compared to a traditional push-based CI/CD pipeline, which security advantage is characteristic of the pull-based GitOps model?", "options": ["Cluster credentials stay in the cluster rather than in an external CI system", "It eliminates the need for any authentication to the Git repository at all", "It removes the requirement to scan container images for known vulnerabilities", "It automatically encrypts all Kubernetes Secrets at rest inside etcd storage"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Because an in-cluster agent pulls changes, the cluster's write credentials never have to be handed to an external CI system, reducing the attack surface. GitOps does not remove the need for Git authentication, image scanning, or separate Secret encryption.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-19", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Fundamentals", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "In the Helm ecosystem, what is the standard term for the packaged unit that bundles all the Kubernetes resource templates and metadata needed to deploy an application?", "options": ["A chart", "A manifest", "A pod", "A kustomization"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A Helm chart is the packaging format that contains templated Kubernetes manifests plus metadata (Chart.yaml, values.yaml, templates/). A 'manifest' is just a rendered YAML resource definition, a 'pod' is a single workload object, and 'kustomization' belongs to the Kustomize tool, not Helm.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-20", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Values", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "How does Helm allow a user to customize a chart's configuration without editing the chart's templates directly?", "options": ["By overriding parameters in a values file or with --set flags", "By editing the rendered manifests stored in etcd directly", "By patching each resource with a separate kubectl command", "By recompiling the chart binary with new default settings"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Helm separates configuration from templates: defaults live in values.yaml and users override them via a custom values file (-f) or --set on the command line. Charts are not compiled binaries, etcd is not edited directly, and customization happens at render time rather than by manually patching live objects.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/values_files/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-21", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Kustomize Approach", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which statement best describes how Kustomize differs from Helm in its core approach to customizing Kubernetes manifests?", "options": ["Kustomize patches plain YAML with template-free overlays; Helm uses a templating engine", "Kustomize requires Go templates for every field; Helm applies plain YAML patch files", "Kustomize can only delete existing resources; Helm is the only one that can create them", "Kustomize runs exclusively inside the cluster; Helm runs only on the client machine"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Kustomize is template-free: it takes valid base YAML and applies declarative overlays/patches without a templating language. Helm, by contrast, relies on Go-template-based variable substitution driven by values. Both are client-side build/render tools, and neither is limited to only creating or only deleting resources.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/guides/introduction/kustomize/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-22", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Kustomize Bases and Overlays", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A team keeps one common set of manifests and then creates separate folders that patch them for dev, staging, and production. Which Kustomize concept are the per-environment folders an example of?", "options": ["Overlays that reference and modify a common base", "Helm subcharts pulled from a dependency repository", "CustomResourceDefinitions for each environment", "Immutable ConfigMaps generated at runtime"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Kustomize uses a base (shared manifests) plus overlays (environment-specific kustomizations that reference the base via 'resources' and apply patches). Subcharts are a Helm dependency mechanism, and CRDs extend the Kubernetes API rather than implement this layering pattern.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/glossary/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-23", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Kubectl and Kustomize Integration", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Kustomize functionality is built directly into kubectl. Which command applies a directory containing a kustomization.yaml to a cluster?", "options": ["kubectl apply -k <directory>", "kubectl kustomize install <directory>", "kubectl helm apply <directory>", "kubectl create chart <directory>"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Kustomize is integrated into kubectl, and the -k flag tells kubectl to build a kustomization directory and apply the result. ('kubectl kustomize <dir>' only renders to stdout without applying.) There is no 'kubectl helm' or 'kubectl create chart' subcommand.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-kubernetes-objects/kustomization/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-24", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Releases and Lifecycle", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "When Helm installs a chart into a cluster, it creates a named release and stores its state so the deployment can be upgraded or rolled back later. Where does modern Helm (v3) store this release state by default?", "options": ["In Kubernetes Secrets in the release's namespace", "In a dedicated Tiller pod running in kube-system", "In a local file on the operator's workstation", "In the chart's values.yaml committed to Git"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Helm v3 persists release information as Secrets (base64-encoded, type helm.sh/release.v1) in the target namespace by default, enabling upgrades and rollbacks. Tiller was removed in Helm v3, so there is no server-side Tiller component, and release state is not kept solely on the workstation or in values.yaml.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/topics/advanced/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-25", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI/CD Fundamentals", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "In a CI/CD pipeline for a containerized app, what is the build stage's main output?", "options": ["A running Pod already serving production traffic in the cluster", "An immutable container image pushed to a registry with a tag", "A set of Kubernetes RBAC roles granting the pipeline access", "A rendered Helm values file committed back to the Git repo"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The build stage compiles the application and packages it into an immutable container image, which is pushed to a registry. Deployment happens in a later stage; RBAC and values files are inputs to delivery, not build outputs.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-26", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Artifact Immutability", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Why do delivery pipelines prefer referencing an image by digest rather than by a mutable tag when promoting a release between environments?", "options": ["Digests are shorter than tags, so manifests stay easier to read", "Tags cannot be used by Kubernetes once an image leaves the build", "A digest always resolves to the same bits, so promotion is exact", "Only digests can be scanned for vulnerabilities by a registry"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "A tag can be overwritten in the registry, so the same tag may resolve to different bits later. A digest is content-addressed and immutable, guaranteeing the artifact tested in staging is exactly the one deployed to production.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-27", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Deployment Strategies", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which description matches a blue/green deployment?", "options": ["Two full environments run side by side and traffic switches over at once", "A small share of traffic shifts to the new version and grows gradually", "Pods are replaced a few at a time until every replica is updated", "All old Pods stop before any new Pods are created, causing downtime"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Blue/green keeps two complete environments, with the router cutting traffic from the old (blue) to the new (green) in one step and back if needed. Gradual traffic shifting is a canary, incremental replacement is a rolling update, and stopping all Pods first is the Recreate strategy.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/blue-green-deployment/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-28", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Deployment Strategies", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A team routes 5% of live traffic to a new release, watches error rates, then increases the share. Which strategy is this?", "options": ["A Recreate deployment that replaces the workload in one step", "A blue/green deployment with two parallel environments", "A rollback to the previously known-good application revision", "A canary release that grows exposure as confidence rises"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "A canary release exposes a small percentage of real traffic to the new version and expands it as metrics stay healthy, limiting blast radius. Blue/green switches all at once, Recreate replaces everything, and a rollback moves backward.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/canary-deployment/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-29", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Progressive Delivery", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Tools such as Argo Rollouts and Flagger add progressive delivery to Kubernetes. What do they primarily automate?", "options": ["Building container images from source whenever a commit lands", "Gradual traffic shifting with automated analysis and rollback", "Encrypting Secrets before they are committed to a Git repository", "Provisioning the cluster nodes that the new release will run on"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Progressive delivery controllers automate canary and blue/green rollouts: they shift traffic in steps, evaluate metrics against success criteria, and roll back automatically when analysis fails. Image building, secret encryption, and node provisioning are separate concerns.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-30", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Environment Promotion", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "In a GitOps workflow with dev, staging, and production, what does 'promoting' a release usually mean in practice?", "options": ["Rebuilding the application image separately for each environment", "Granting the deployment service account broader RBAC permissions", "Updating the environment's manifests in Git to the tested version", "Manually running kubectl apply against the production cluster"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Promotion means changing the desired state in Git for the target environment to point at the already-built, already-tested artifact; the GitOps agent then reconciles the cluster. Rebuilding per environment breaks the build-once, promote-the-same-artifact rule.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-31", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Principles", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "The OpenGitOps project defines a set of core principles. Which statement reflects one of them?", "options": ["The desired state is declarative and versioned immutably in Git", "The desired state is stored as a sequence of imperative commands", "Only cluster administrators may view the desired state of a system", "The desired state is kept exclusively inside the cluster's etcd"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "OpenGitOps defines four principles: the desired state is declarative, versioned and immutable, pulled automatically by software agents, and continuously reconciled. Imperative command sequences and cluster-only state are the opposite of the model.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-32", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Push vs Pull", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What distinguishes a pull-based GitOps deployment from a push-based CI/CD deployment?", "options": ["Pull-based pipelines must run outside the cluster on a CI runner", "An in-cluster agent fetches desired state instead of CI pushing it", "Pull-based delivery works only for stateless application workloads", "Push-based delivery cannot deploy to more than one cluster at a time"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "In the pull model an agent inside the cluster watches Git and applies changes itself; in the push model an external CI system holds cluster credentials and pushes manifests in. Both models can handle stateful workloads and multiple clusters.", "sourceUrl": "https://fluxcd.io/flux/concepts/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-33", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Repository Structure", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A team wants one Git repository to describe dev, staging, and production without duplicating every manifest. Which approach fits GitOps best?", "options": ["Commit the rendered manifests for each cluster to a separate branch", "Store only production manifests and edit them by hand per cluster", "Keep each environment in its own repository with no shared content", "Keep a shared base and per-environment overlays in separate paths"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "A shared base with per-environment overlays (Kustomize) or one chart with per-environment values (Helm) keeps a single source of truth while varying only what differs. Duplicating manifests across branches or repos lets environments drift apart.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/glossary/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-34", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Secrets", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Storing plain Kubernetes Secrets in a Git repository is unsafe. Which approach keeps GitOps workable while protecting secret material?", "options": ["Base64-encode the Secret manifests before committing them to Git", "Grant the GitOps agent cluster-admin so it can create Secrets itself", "Commit encrypted secrets only the cluster can decrypt at apply time", "Keep secrets in Git but make the repository private to the team"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tools such as Sealed Secrets and SOPS encrypt the secret before it is committed, so the repository holds ciphertext and only an in-cluster controller holds the decryption key. Base64 is encoding rather than encryption, and a private repo still stores plaintext.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-35", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Argo CD Application", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "In Argo CD, what does an Application resource define?", "options": ["The source repository and the cluster destination to sync it to", "The container image build steps run before a deployment starts", "The RBAC roles granted to developers inside the target namespace", "The network policy applied between the synced application's Pods"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "An Argo CD Application declares where the manifests come from (repository, path, revision) and where they should be applied (cluster and namespace), plus its sync policy. It does not build images or define RBAC and network policy for the workload.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/declarative-setup/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-36", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Argo CD Sync Waves", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "An application needs its CustomResourceDefinitions applied before the workloads that use them. Which Argo CD feature expresses that ordering?", "options": ["Setting the application's sync policy to automated with self-heal", "Sync waves, which apply resources in an explicit ordered sequence", "A diff customization that ignores the CRD fields during comparison", "An app-of-apps pattern that nests one Application inside another"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Sync waves annotate resources with an order so Argo CD applies lower waves first and waits for them to become healthy. Automated sync controls when a sync happens, diff customization controls comparison, and app-of-apps groups Applications rather than ordering resources.", "sourceUrl": "https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/sync-waves/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-37", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Flux Components", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Flux is built as a set of controllers rather than one binary. What does its source controller do?", "options": ["It applies rendered manifests to the cluster and prunes removed ones", "It notifies external systems when a reconciliation run has finished", "It scans running workloads for newer container image tags to deploy", "It fetches and verifies artifacts from Git, OCI, and Helm repositories"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The source controller acquires artifacts from Git repositories, OCI registries, and Helm repositories and makes them available to the other controllers. Applying manifests is the kustomize controller's job, notifications belong to the notification controller, and tag scanning to the image automation controllers.", "sourceUrl": "https://fluxcd.io/flux/components/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-38", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Audit Trail", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Which operational benefit follows directly from Git being the source of truth for cluster state?", "options": ["Container images no longer need to be scanned for vulnerabilities", "The cluster can run without any authentication on the API server", "Every change carries an author, timestamp, and reviewable history", "Applications automatically scale in response to incoming traffic"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Because every desired-state change is a commit, the repository provides a complete audit trail and a review point before anything reaches the cluster, and reverting is a git revert. Scanning, authentication, and autoscaling are unrelated concerns.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-39", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Repositories", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "What is a Helm repository?", "options": ["A running controller in the cluster that renders charts on demand", "An HTTP server hosting packaged charts and an index of them", "A Git branch that holds the rendered output of a chart install", "A registry that stores only container images used by a chart"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A Helm repository is an HTTP server serving packaged chart archives alongside an index.yaml describing them, which the Helm client adds and searches. Charts are rendered by the client, not by an in-cluster controller.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/topics/chart_repository/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-40", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Dependencies", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A chart needs a database chart installed alongside it. How does Helm express that relationship?", "options": ["As a dependency declared in Chart.yaml and fetched into charts/", "As an initContainer added to every Pod template in the chart", "As a separate values file that the operator must apply first", "As a Kubernetes CustomResourceDefinition shipped by the chart"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Dependencies are listed in the chart's Chart.yaml and pulled into the charts/ subdirectory by helm dependency update, so the parent chart installs them together. Init containers, values files, and CRDs do not express chart-level dependencies.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-41", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Rollback", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A Helm upgrade leaves an application broken. What does helm rollback do?", "options": ["It deletes the release and reinstalls the chart from scratch again", "It reverts the chart source in Git to the previous commit that worked", "It restores the release to a previous stored revision of its state", "It pauses the release so no further upgrades can be applied to it"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Helm records each install and upgrade as a numbered revision, so rollback re-applies a previous revision's manifests. It does not touch Git, delete the release, or lock it against further changes.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_rollback/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-42", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm Templating", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What makes a Helm chart reusable across different environments?", "options": ["Each environment gets its own copy of the chart's template files", "The chart is rendered once and the output is edited per environment", "Kubernetes substitutes environment variables into the manifests itself", "Templates reference values that are supplied per install or upgrade"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "Chart templates contain placeholders resolved from values, so one chart serves many environments by supplying different values files or --set flags. Copying or hand-editing rendered output defeats the purpose, and Kubernetes does not substitute values into manifests.", "sourceUrl": "https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/values_files/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-43", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Kustomize Patches", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "An overlay must change only the replica count of a Deployment defined in the base. What does Kustomize use to express that?", "options": ["A full copy of the Deployment manifest with the new replica count", "A patch that specifies only the fields being changed in the base", "A Go template conditional evaluated when the overlay is rendered", "An annotation that the kustomize controller reads at apply time"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Kustomize applies patches that list only the differing fields, merging them onto the base resource, which keeps overlays small and the base authoritative. Kustomize is template-free, so Go template conditionals do not apply.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/patches/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-44", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Kustomize Generators", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Why does configMapGenerator append a hash suffix to the ConfigMap names it creates?", "options": ["So changing config produces a new name and triggers a rollout", "So the ConfigMap can be shared safely across many namespaces", "So Kubernetes can validate the ConfigMap contents before applying", "So the ConfigMap is excluded from the cluster's garbage collection"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The content hash makes the generated name change whenever the data changes, so referencing Pods see a new ConfigMap name and the Deployment rolls out instead of silently keeping stale values. It is not about sharing, validation, or garbage collection.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/configmapgenerator/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-45", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Helm vs Kustomize", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A team wants to customize third-party manifests without learning a templating language. Which tool matches that preference?", "options": ["Helm, because charts avoid templating in favor of plain YAML", "Helm, because values files replace the need for any patching", "Kustomize, because overlays patch plain YAML without templates", "Kustomize, because it renders Go templates into final manifests"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Kustomize is template-free: it patches plain YAML through bases and overlays. Helm is the templating option, using Go templates driven by values, so the choices attributing templating to Kustomize or plain YAML to Helm are reversed.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/guides/introduction/kustomize/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-46", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Custom Resource Definitions", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What does creating a CustomResourceDefinition add to a cluster?", "options": ["A controller that reconciles the new resource type automatically", "A new API resource type the API server will store and serve", "A webhook that validates every existing built-in resource type", "A namespace dedicated to holding the new custom resource objects"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A CRD extends the Kubernetes API with a new resource type so objects of that kind can be created and retrieved like built-ins. The behavior comes from a separate controller — a CRD on its own stores data and does nothing with it.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definitions/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-47", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Operator Pattern", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "What distinguishes an Operator from a plain CustomResourceDefinition?", "options": ["An Operator is a CRD that Kubernetes validates more strictly", "An Operator replaces the built-in controllers in the control plane", "An Operator is a CRD that can only be installed cluster-wide", "An Operator pairs a custom resource with a controller acting on it"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The Operator pattern combines a custom resource, which describes desired state, with a controller that encodes operational knowledge and reconciles toward it. A CRD on its own only extends the API; it does not act.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-48", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Operator Day-2 Operations", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Operators are often described as automating 'Day-2' operations. Which task is a Day-2 concern?", "options": ["Taking scheduled backups and running version upgrades in place", "Choosing which container base image the application will use", "Writing the initial Deployment manifest for the application", "Deciding how many worker nodes the new cluster should have"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Day-1 is initial installation and configuration; Day-2 covers ongoing operations such as backups, upgrades, failover, and scaling, which is the knowledge an Operator encodes. Image choice, first manifests, and cluster sizing are earlier decisions.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-49", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Image Signing", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "How does signing a container image improve delivery security?", "options": ["It compresses the image so that fewer bytes cross the network", "It encrypts the image layers so the registry cannot read them", "It lets a verifier confirm the image's origin and integrity", "It removes known vulnerabilities from the image's base layers"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "A signature lets a consumer verify cryptographically that the image came from the expected builder and has not been altered since. Signing does not compress, encrypt, or patch the image — scanning and rebuilding address vulnerabilities.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-50", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Software Bill of Materials", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "What does an SBOM provide for a delivered container image?", "options": ["A record of which clusters the image has been deployed onto", "An inventory of the components and dependencies it contains", "A signature proving the image was built by a trusted pipeline", "A performance profile of the application running in the image"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A Software Bill of Materials lists the packages, libraries, and versions inside an artifact, so teams can answer which images are affected when a vulnerability is disclosed. Provenance signatures and deployment records are separate artifacts.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/cloud-native-security/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-51", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Admission Control in Delivery", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "A cluster must refuse any image that is not signed by the organization's build system. Where is that rule enforced?", "options": ["By an admission controller checking images as Pods are created", "By the container runtime when it pulls the image onto the node", "By the CI pipeline immediately after it finishes building it", "By the Git repository rejecting the commit that references it"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Admission controllers intercept requests to the API server before objects are persisted, so a policy can reject Pods referencing unsigned images cluster-wide. A CI check can be bypassed by applying manifests directly, and runtimes and Git do not evaluate signature policy.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-52", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Registry Authentication", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "A Deployment fails with ImagePullBackOff against a private registry. Which object supplies the credentials?", "options": ["A ConfigMap referenced by the Pod's environment variables", "A ServiceAccount token mounted into the running container", "A NetworkPolicy permitting egress to the registry endpoint", "An imagePullSecret referenced by the Pod or ServiceAccount"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "Registry credentials live in a Secret of type kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson and are referenced as an imagePullSecret on the Pod or its ServiceAccount. ConfigMaps hold non-confidential data, and service account tokens authenticate to the API server rather than a registry.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-53", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Pipeline Artifacts", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Why should a delivery pipeline build an artifact once and reuse it across environments?", "options": ["Because registries charge for each separate image build performed", "Because Kubernetes refuses to run an image built more than once", "Because rebuilding risks shipping bits that were never tested", "Because a rebuilt image cannot be signed a second time by policy"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Rebuilding per environment can pick up different dependencies or base layers, so what reaches production is not what passed the tests. Building once and promoting the same digest keeps the tested artifact and the released artifact identical.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-54", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Rollback Strategy", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A Deployment rollout is failing readiness checks in production. Which response restores service fastest?", "options": ["Scale the Deployment up so healthy replicas outnumber failing ones", "Roll back the Deployment to the previous working revision", "Delete the namespace and re-apply every manifest from scratch", "Remove the readiness probe so the new Pods are marked ready"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Kubernetes keeps prior ReplicaSets, so rolling back returns to a known-good revision immediately. Scaling up adds more failing Pods, deleting the namespace causes an outage, and removing the probe hides the failure while still serving broken Pods.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#rolling-back-a-deployment", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-55", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "CI Feedback Loop", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Why do CI systems run the test suite on every merge rather than once before release?", "options": ["Defects are cheaper to fix the sooner after the change they surface", "Running tests more often makes each individual test run faster", "Kubernetes requires a passing test run before it accepts manifests", "Test suites expire and must be re-run to stay valid for release"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Frequent automated testing keeps the feedback loop short, so a breaking change is caught while the context is fresh and the change set is small. Kubernetes has no knowledge of test results, and test suites do not expire.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-integration/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-56", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Declarative Delivery", "difficulty": "easy", "question": "Why is a declarative manifest better suited to repeatable delivery than a sequence of kubectl commands?", "options": ["Manifests execute faster than the equivalent imperative commands", "The same manifest can be re-applied to converge on one end state", "Manifests are the only format the Kubernetes API server accepts", "Imperative commands cannot create Deployments or Services at all"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A declarative manifest describes the end state, so applying it repeatedly is safe and produces the same result regardless of the starting point. Imperative commands can create resources, but replaying them depends on the current state.", "sourceUrl": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-kubernetes-objects/declarative-config/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-57", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Immutable Infrastructure", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "An operator fixes a production bug by exec-ing into a container and editing a file. Why does this break immutable delivery?", "options": ["The container will refuse to restart after its filesystem changes", "Kubernetes blocks exec access to containers running in production", "The change is lost only if the node hosting the Pod is drained", "The change vanishes on restart and exists in no image or manifest"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "A Pod restart or reschedule recreates the container from its image, discarding the edit, and nothing in Git or the registry records it — so environments silently diverge. The correct path is a new image built from a committed change.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/immutable-infrastructure/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-58", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "GitOps Reconciliation Interval", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "A GitOps controller is configured with automated sync and a reconciliation interval. What does the interval control?", "options": ["How often the agent compares cluster state against the Git repo", "How long the agent waits before deleting resources from a cluster", "How frequently the CI system rebuilds the application's image", "How often the Git repository history is compacted and pruned"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The interval sets how often the agent re-reads the desired state and compares it against live state, applying changes when they differ. It does not govern image builds or repository maintenance.", "sourceUrl": "https://fluxcd.io/flux/concepts/", "holdout": true}, {"id": "delivery-q-59", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Continuous Integration Practice", "difficulty": "medium", "question": "Which practice most directly supports continuous integration?", "options": ["Keeping feature work on long-lived branches until it is complete", "Batching a quarter of changes into a single large release window", "Merging small, tested changes into the mainline branch frequently", "Deferring all automated testing to a pre-release stabilization phase"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Continuous integration depends on small changes reaching a shared mainline often, so conflicts and regressions surface quickly. Long-lived branches, batched releases, and deferred testing all lengthen the feedback loop.", "sourceUrl": "https://glossary.cncf.io/continuous-integration/"}, {"id": "delivery-q-60", "domainId": "delivery", "topic": "Pipeline Security", "difficulty": "hard", "question": "Why is a push-based pipeline holding long-lived cluster credentials considered a larger risk than a pull-based agent?", "options": ["External CI systems cannot use short-lived credentials at all", "A compromise of the CI system yields direct access to the cluster", "Push pipelines must disable TLS when talking to the API server", "Cluster credentials stop working once they leave the cluster network"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "In the push model the CI system stores credentials that can write to the cluster, so compromising CI compromises the cluster. A pull-based agent keeps credentials inside the cluster and only needs read access to the repository.", "sourceUrl": "https://opengitops.dev/", "holdout": true}]});
